v.org/abs/math/0410004\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 157\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Combinatorics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 30 Sep 2004 (【5†v1】), last revised 15 Aug 2006 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:The Symmetric Subset Problem in Continuous Ramsey Theory\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Greg Martin】, 【7†Kevin O'Bryant】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled The Symmetric Subset Problem in Continuous Ramsey Theory, by Greg Martin and Kevin O'Bryant\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract: A symmetric subset of the reals is one that remains invariant under some reflection z --> c-z. We consider, for any 0 < x <= 1, the largest real number D(x) such that every subset of $[0,1]$ with measure greater than x contains a symmetric subset with measure D(x). In this paper we establish upper and lower bounds for D(x) of the same order of magnitude: for example, we prove that D(x) = 2x - 1 for 11/16 <= x <= 1 and that 0.59 x^2 < D(x) < 0.8 x^2 for 0 < x <= 11/16.\nL17: > This continuous problem is intimately connected with a corresponding discrete problem. A set S of integers is called a B*[g] set if for any given m there are at most g ordered pairs (s_1,s_2) \\in S \\times S with s_1+s_2 = m; in the case g=2, these are better known as Sidon sets. Our lower bound on D(x) implies that every B*[g] set contained in \\{1,2,...,n\\} has cardinality less than 1.30036 \\sqrt{gn}. This improves a result of Green for g >= 30.\nL18: Conversely, we use a probabilistic construction of B*[g] sets to establish an upper bound on D(x) for small x.\nL19: Comments:  | 36 pages. to appear in Experimental Mathematics\nL20: Subjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO); Number Theory (math.NT)\nL21: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 05D99; 42A16; 11B83\nL22: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:math/0410004】 [math.CO]\nL23:    | (or 【11†arXiv:math/0410004v2】 [math.CO] for this version)\nL24:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.math/0410004†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: Journal reference:  | Experiment. Math. 16 (2007), no. 2, 145--165.\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Kevin O'Bryant [【13†view email】]\nL29: 【5†[v1]】 Thu, 30 Sep 2004 22:26:20 UTC (68 KB)\nL30: [v2] Tue, 15 Aug 2006 05:04:37 UTC (68 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled The Symmetric Subset Problem in Continuous Ramsey Theory, by Greg Martin and Kevin O'Bryant\nL37: \nL38:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL39:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL40:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL41: \nL42: 【15†view license】\nL43: \nL44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: math.CO\nL47: \nL48: 【16†< prev】   |   【17†next >】\nL49: \nL50: 【18†new】 | 【19†recent】 | 【20†2004-10】\nL51: ### References & Citations\nL52: \nL53:   * 【21†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL54:   * 【22†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL55:   * 【23†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL56: \nL57: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL58: \nL59: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL60: \nL61: [Button: ×]\nL62: \nL63: loading...\nL64: \nL65: Data provided by:\nL66: \nL67: ### Bookmark\nL68: \nL69: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL70: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL73: \nL74: Bibliographic Explorer (【24†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL77: \nL78: Connected Papers (【25†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com】)\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL81: \nL82: Litmaps (【26†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co】)\nL83: \nL84: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL85: \nL86: scite Smart Citations (【27†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai】)\nL87: \nL88: [Input] Code, Data, Media\nL89: # Code, Data and Media Associated with this Article