XivLabs†info.arxiv.org】.\nL152: \nL153: 【40†Which authors of this paper are endorsers?】 | Disable MathJax (【41†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL154: \nL155: We gratefully acknowledge support from our 【42†major funders†info.arxiv.org】, 【43†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\nL156: 【44†About†info.arxiv.org】 · 【45†Help†info.arxiv.org】 · 【46†Contact†info.arxiv.org】 · 【47†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org】 · 【48†Copyright†info.arxiv.org】 · 【49†Privacy†info.arxiv.org】 · 【50†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org】 · 【51†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[math/0210041] Continuous Ramsey Theory and Sidon Sets (https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0210041)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0210041\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Number Theory\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 3 Oct 2002 (【5†v1】), last revised 5 Oct 2002 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Continuous Ramsey Theory and Sidon Sets\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Greg Martin】, 【7†Kevin O'Bryant】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Continuous Ramsey Theory and Sidon Sets, by Greg Martin and Kevin O'Bryant\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract: A symmetric subset of the reals is one that remains invariant under some reflection x --> c-x. Given 0 < x < 1, there exists a real number D(x) with the following property: if 0 < d < D(x), then every subset of [0,1] with measure x contains a symmetric subset with measure d, while if d > D(x), then there exists a subset of [0,1] with measure x that does not contain a symmetric subset with measure d.\nL17: In this paper we establish upper and lower bounds for D(x) of the same order of magnitude: for example, we prove that D(x) = 2x - 1 for 11/16 < x < 1 and that 0.59 x^2 < D(x) < 0.8 x^2 for 0 < x < 11/16.\nL18: > This continuous problem is intimately connected with a corresponding discrete problem. A set S of integers is called a B*[g] set if for any given m there are at most g ordered pairs (s_1,s_2) \\in S \\times S with s_1+s_2=m; in the case g=2, these are better known as Sidon sets. We also establish upper and lower bounds of the same order of magnitude for the maximal possible size of a B*[g] set contained in {1,...,n}, which we denote by R(g,n).\nL19: For example, we prove that R(g,n) < 1.31 \\sqrt{gn} for all n > g > 1, while R(g,n) > 0.79 \\sqrt{gn} for sufficiently large integers g and n.\nL20: > These two problems are so interconnected that both continuous and discrete tools can be applied to each problem with surprising effectiveness. The harmonic analysis methods and inequalities among various L^p norms we use to derive lower bounds for D(x) also provide uniform upper bounds for R(g,n), while the techniques from combinatorial and probabilistic number theory that we employ to obtain constructions of large B*[g] sets yield strong upper bounds for D(x).\nL21: Comments:  | 66 pages, 8 figures, 3 tables; formatting improved\nL22: Subjects:  | Number Theory (math.NT); Combinatorics (math.CO)\nL23: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 11P70\nL24: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:math/0210041】 [math.NT]\nL25:    | (or 【11†arXiv:math/0210041v2】 [math.NT] for this version)\nL26:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.math/0210041†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL27: ## Submission history\nL28: \nL29: From: Kevin O'Bryant [【13†view email】]\nL30: 【5†[v1]】 Thu, 3 Oct 2002 02:22:08 UTC (113 KB)\nL31: [v2] Sat, 5 Oct 2002 18:43:59 UTC (113 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: \nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Continuous Ramsey